---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CustomisedBreadCrumbs from "../components/CustomisedBreadCrumbs.astro";

const thread = [
  {
    href: "/projects/asd-myelin-timing",
    kicker: "Myelin",
    line: "Investigating myelination and autism using human genetic data.",
  },
  {
    href: "/projects/anesthesia-pipeline",
    kicker: "Anesthesia and sleep",
    line: "Exploring reversible changes in nervous-system activity.",
  },
  {
    href: "/projects/libribrain-2026",
    kicker: "LibriBrain",
    line: "MEG decoding and the influence of timing on model evaluation.",
  },
  {
    href: "/projects/handwriting-emg",
    kicker: "Handwriting",
    line: "Adapting a wrist-EMG handwriting decoder to individual writers.",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — Rohit Ravi"
  description="How illness, philosophy, and a search for understanding led Rohit Ravi to work in machine learning, neuroscience, and computation."
>
  <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 py-10">
    <CustomisedBreadCrumbs />
    <header
      class="mt-8 grid items-end gap-8 md:grid-cols-[17rem_minmax(0,1fr)]"
    >
      <figure class="mx-auto w-full max-w-xs md:mx-0 md:max-w-none">
        <img
          src="/images/about/rohit-ravi.jpg"
          alt="Rohit Ravi, in a navy suit, standing between stone columns."
          width="1024"
          height="1536"
          class="aspect-[3/4] w-full rounded-lg object-cover object-[center_18%] shadow-md"
        />
        <figcaption
          class="mt-2 text-center text-xs text-muted-foreground md:text-left"
        >
          Rohit Ravi
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div>
        <p
          class="text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-muted-foreground"
        >
          About
        </p>
        <h1
          class="mt-2 scroll-m-20 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl"
        >
          Rohit Ravi
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed">
          I study nervous systems because I know how profoundly they can change
          a life—and how much remains unexplained when they do.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          I’m studying data science and philosophy at NYU, with research
          interests spanning machine learning, neural computation, and biology.
          Those interests developed through coursework, but their urgency came
          from a severe illness that began during college. What started as a
          search for answers about my own health grew into a broader commitment:
          to develop the technical understanding needed to help people whose
          suffering we still struggle to explain.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>
    <div class="mt-12 flex flex-col gap-12 text-base leading-relaxed">
      <section>
        <h2 class="border-t pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
          Before the questions became personal
        </h2>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
          <p>
            For much of my life, I understood progress through training. I
            played four years of varsity tennis at Northern Highlands, and
            during the COVID lockdown I often spent six hours a day on court.
            Fitness, energy, and the ability to push myself were central to my
            daily life. I expected to keep building on them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Becoming ill disrupted that continuity. Activities that had once
            been routine became inaccessible. I lost the physical capacity I had
            depended on, and long stretches of illness narrowed the world around
            me.
          </p>
          <p>
            Treatment eventually made the condition more manageable. I’m
            grateful for the clinicians who helped make that possible. Yet
            relief and explanation were different things: the underlying cause
            remained uncertain, even after consultations across specialties. I
            was left with questions that mattered beyond any single appointment.
            Where was the dysfunction occurring? What could explain such a large
            change in experience and ability when conventional investigations
            offered so little clarity?
          </p>
          <p>
            I began reading because I wanted answers. Over time, I wanted the
            skills to investigate the questions myself.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="border-t pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
          Finding a direction
        </h2>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
          <p>
            Alongside that search, I began exploring my roots and identity more
            seriously. Vedanta became a source of orientation during a period
            when much of what had previously defined my life was unavailable to
            me. Its questions about suffering, selfhood, and consciousness had
            an immediate personal significance.
          </p>
          <p>
            That exploration remains an important part of my life. It also
            deepened my interest in philosophy of mind: how subjective
            experience relates to physical processes, what we mean by a self,
            and what an explanation of consciousness would actually need to
            explain. I want to examine those questions carefully, including
            where philosophical reasoning and empirical investigation can inform
            each other and where their methods differ.
          </p>
          <p>
            My sense of how I wanted to help people changed during this period.
            I came to see research and engineering as the avenue through which I
            could contribute most. Machine learning, neuroscience, and
            computation offered ways to investigate mechanisms, build useful
            tools, and work on problems whose consequences extend across many
            lives.
          </p>
          <p>
            The uncertainty that had been so difficult to live with became a
            reason to learn.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="border-t pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
          How that became my work
        </h2>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
          <p>
            My coursework in machine learning gave me a practical starting
            point. Working with <em>C. elegans</em> let me begin studying nervous
            systems computationally: how connectivity, cellular properties, and activity
            relate, and what a model can reasonably tell us about the living system
            it represents. The notebooks were a learning pipeline, through which
            I developed the ability to turn broad biological questions into analyses
            and experiments.
          </p>
          <p>
            My own search also drew me toward genetic, mitochondrial, and
            myelination mechanisms. I was interested in how nervous-system
            function can be disrupted through processes that are difficult to
            see in a conventional structural investigation.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some of that reading informed things I tried personally, including
            DHA and palmitoylethanolamide with luteolin. I experienced benefits,
            although that experience cannot establish which mechanism was
            responsible or what would help someone else. It gave me further
            questions to investigate.
          </p>
          <p>
            The autism and myelination work became especially personal when I
            saw a young relative suffering. I recognized something of the
            helplessness I had experienced myself, and I wanted to help in
            whatever way I could. My experience with DHA was part of what first
            directed my attention toward lipid supply and myelination. From
            there, the task was to examine whether the biological evidence
            supported the initial idea—and how the question needed to change
            when it did not.
          </p>
          <p>
            My interest in anesthesia and sleep grew from another observation
            during treatment: profound changes in nervous-system state could
            interrupt acute suffering. That made reversibility especially
            compelling to me. How can a nervous system enter a dramatically
            different state and then return? What distinguishes reduced
            activity, quiescence, sleep, and anesthesia? Computational models
            offered a way to begin exploring those questions.
          </p>
          <p>
            These projects share a personal origin, but each needs evidence
            appropriate to its own claims. My experience helps explain why I ask
            the questions. The research has to determine what answers they can
            support.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="border-t pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
          From biological questions to neural data
        </h2>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
          <p>
            As my technical skills developed, my work expanded into decoding
            information from neural signals.
          </p>
          <p>
            LibriBrain brought me into MEG analysis and the challenge of
            extracting useful information from noisy measurements. It also made
            evaluation itself a central part of the work: understanding how much
            of a model’s performance reflects the neural signal and how much can
            be explained by timing or other features of the task.
          </p>
          <p>
            The wrist-EMG handwriting project explores a different interface
            between nervous systems and computation. It asks how signals
            associated with intended movement can support communication, and how
            adaptation to an individual changes a model’s usefulness.
          </p>
          <p>
            These projects are opportunities to demonstrate the engineering
            skills behind my broader interests: working with complex data,
            training and adapting models, designing evaluations, and
            investigating why a result changes. They also connect to something
            that has become deeply important to me—how computation can help
            preserve or recover a person’s ability to act, communicate, and
            participate in life.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
      <nav class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2" aria-label="Explore the research">
        {
          thread.map((item) => (
            <a
              href={item.href}
              class="rounded-lg border bg-card px-4 py-3 transition hover:border-primary"
            >
              <span class="block text-sm font-semibold text-primary">
                {item.kicker}
              </span>
              <span class="mt-1 block text-sm leading-snug text-muted-foreground">
                {item.line}
              </span>
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </nav>
      <section>
        <h2 class="border-t pt-8 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
          What I want to contribute
        </h2>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-4">
          <p>
            I want to bring this combination of personal commitment and
            technical ability to a research team or company working on
            consequential problems in biology, neural systems, or machine
            learning.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is much I still want to learn. I’m looking for work that
            demands depth: difficult data, mechanisms that remain uncertain, and
            tools that need to function beyond a demonstration. I want
            collaborators who care about both the quality of an explanation and
            what it could make possible for people.
          </p>
          <p>
            Illness changed the life I expected to have. It also helped clarify
            the work I want to devote myself to. My goal is to use machine
            learning, neuroscience, computation, and philosophy to tackle the
            hardest problems I can contribute to solving—and, through that work,
            make suffering more bearable.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
